WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.570
The last time we talked, I started to walk
you through the nervous system a little bit

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:38.400
and we started with the monkey business illusion,
and one of the things that's really remarkable

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.660
about the monkey business illusion is that--and
change blindness illusions in general--is

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:51.000
that demonstrate to you just to what degree
you could either describe yourself as blind

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.199
or focused.

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:55.410
And I think focused is actually better than
blind.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.270
You know, a laser beam doesn't cover much
area, but it really illuminates what it strikes.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:05.900
And people are sort of like that, we're really
beamed into a single point and there's something

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:12.640
metaphysically spectacular about that, I think,
because that single point that we're narrowed

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.420
down to is in some sense the thing that writes
the world, you know, because as we interact

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.049
with the world we're able to turn it one way
or another in all sorts of complicated ways.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:28.300
And, I mean, I know our powers of doing so
are obviously constrained to a substantial

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:34.680
degree, but by the same token, that focal
point of concentration enables us to interact

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:42.390
with the world and then, at least seemingly,
to change it according to our will.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.610
It's like we take a future that's potential,
we interact with it at a focal point, and

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:51.610
then it transforms itself into a past that's
in some sense fixed and real.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.780
So that's pretty strange.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.870
So I guess you don't want to underestimate
the utility of a point.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:04.620
I want to talk to you a little bit first more
about what you're blind to and then what the

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.100
consequences of that are.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:16.849
So, this here is a little diagram of a computer,
believe it or not--a conceptual diagram of

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:22.730
a computer--and it's predicated on...

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.400
I'll tell you a quick story.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.849
If I've told you this story please stop me,
but I don't think I've told you guys this

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:30.989
story.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:39.930
So one day back in about 1987 I was working
on my computer and I was typing some long

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:45.159
essay in DOS and, you really had to back up
in those days because the programs didn't

00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:49.739
do any back up for you and so if the program
shut off and you hadn't been super careful

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:50.739
you were just going to lose all of it.

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:55.980
And so that's what happened--I was typing
when the computer went off and so, I had an

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.010
emotional reaction to that.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:03.379
Right, now an emotional reaction to that is
kind of an interesting event because, I would

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:08.560
say that you're not even working with the
computer until it quits.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.680
And the reason I would say that is because,
if you think about it, when the computer is

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.680
functioning the fact that it's an extraordinarily
complicated device is basically irrelevant

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:17.680
to you.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.579
What you're working with, roughly speaking,
is the screen, but not really, not even the

00:03:22.579 --> 00:03:23.579
screen.

00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:29.129
You're working with maybe a phrase or even
the word that's presented on the screen.

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:37.870
You know, you're typing the word/phrase/sentence/paragraph/essay,
and only out past that domain of consideration

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.200
do you start thinking about the hardware technology
that underlies what you're doing.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.000
You don't want to think about that at all.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.139
When something's working, you don't have to
see it.

00:03:49.139 --> 00:03:51.930
And so that gives you a clue as to consciousness.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:57.620
Consciousness is an error detecting and correcting
phenomena, and you're not conscious of things

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.370
that are going well.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:04.029
You know, which might explain to some degree
why a lot of our life seems to be made up

00:04:04.029 --> 00:04:05.269
of suffering.

00:04:05.269 --> 00:04:10.879
If things are going well, it isn't exactly
like you're happy if things are going well,

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:13.360
you just don't notice when they're going well.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.550
Sometimes if they unexpectedly go well, then
that makes you happy.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:24.180
But otherwise it's just the same old thing,
even if it's amazing.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.210
I was--yesterday--I was eating breakfast with
my son and I was complaining about the fact

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:32.669
that I had to eat canned-smoked tuna because
I don't really like fish, but I have to eat

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:34.940
fish for a variety of different reasons.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.520
And I thought, Jesus, what're you complaining
about?

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.419
I mean, really, do you know what a tuna is
like?

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:45.670
Those things are like small whales, right;
they weigh like 600 pounds, they're impossible

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:46.670
to catch.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:51.690
So there's some poor characters out there
hauling in tuna, which is impossible--and

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.630
then they have to clean them and freeze them
and smoke them and can them--and all I have

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.290
to do is go to the store and pick up this
can of tuna and then I can complain about

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.160
it for breakfast.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:07.790
It's really quite staggering, the fact that
I can ever eat tuna is a complete bloody miracle.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:11.940
But it's not the sort of thing that makes
me happy because I just expect that.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.200
Which is very pathetic, but that's how it
goes.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.980
So anyways, back to the computer.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:22.940
So, it stops working--well, and that's what
I would say I notice the computer.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.380
And there's nothing more annoying then noticing
a computer when you're in the middle of writing

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.220
an essay because, what the hell do you know
about computers?

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.220
Nothing.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.700
What're you going to do?

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:33.940
Turn it on and off?

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.320
Well that's like problem solving process number
1.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.890
It usually works--thank god--but if it doesn't
work you're into either the software or the

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:44.890
hardware.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:49.480
So anyways, I turned it on and off and that
didn't work, and so then I turned on a light

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.140
to see if I could see if something had happened
at the back of it.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:56.980
But the light didn't go on so I thought, aha,
the power probably went out or I blew a fuse,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:01.430
so I went out to see if the fuse was burnt
out, but it wasn't.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:05.300
And then I noticed that all the power in the
house was off, and then I noticed that--I

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.280
went outside to go to the corner store to
get something, I don't remember what, and

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.811
when I went outside I noticed that all the
streetlights were off.

00:06:11.811 --> 00:06:16.960
And then the entire power grid in Montreal
was out.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.460
And then the entire power grid for like half
of Quebec was out.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.720
And a big chunk of Eastern North America.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:23.720
And do you know why?

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.080
Have I told you this story?

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.500
Good.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.090
Because there was a solar flare.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.620
And so the sun is a big hydrogen bomb, right.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:36.050
So I don't know if you guys know this--a cheerful
piece of information for you if you need to

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:40.720
be cheered up--but if you happened to have
a hydrogen bomb and you exploded it--you know

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.140
a hydrogen bomb has an atom bomb for its trigger,
did you guys know that?

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.440
You need to know that because it's important.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.710
Because an atom bomb is a big thing, but it's
just a trigger for a hydrogen bomb, so a hydrogen

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.450
bomb is unimaginably bigger than an atom bomb.

00:06:56.450 --> 00:07:00.900
Anyways, if you blew a hydrogen bomb in the
atmosphere over Central North America, you

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:08.430
could probably wipe out maybe all of the electronic
equipment in the entire continent permanently.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.560
Because what happens is when a hydrogen bomb
goes off, there's an electromagnetic burst,

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.280
and as the propagates it'll hit your electronic
equipment and propagate across the wires and

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.340
produce a big spike in current, voltage I
think, and it'll just blow it.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:27.310
So your cars won't work, your tractors won't
work, your subways won't work, your cars won't

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:30.640
work, your computers won't work, your satellites
won't work.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.030
It's like, done.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:37.590
And also, just to cheer you up even more,
back in the late 1800s, I think it was the

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:43.580
1860s, there was this--oh let's go back to
the sun.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:49.280
So the sun's a big hydrogen bomb and now and
then it freaks out and emits a big solar flare,

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.490
which is something that's gone wrong on the
sun.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:08:01.450
And maybe it blows out this solar flare almost
to the orbit of mercury, and then that burst

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:06.570
of radiation comes zooming towards earth and
then 9 minutes later it hits and, if it's

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:11.190
a decent electromagnetic pulse it'll wipe
out the power grid in Quebec.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.520
And that's what happened.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.130
So the reason my computer didn't work is because
the sun was misbehaving.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:18.780
And so, I like that.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.840
It was very illuminating to me because it
just shows you how many things need to be

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:28.530
stacked on top of each other, working perfectly
for some thing that you're doing to actually

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:29.530
function.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:34.419
So anyways, back in the late 1800s in the
1860s, there was a massive solar flare and

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:41.010
it produced a bust of power on telegraph lines
and it lit some telegraph operators on fire.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:44.670
And so this does happen--it happens about
once a century, and we just missed one last

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.700
year.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:52.010
So while you're worrying about global warming
you can be considering the much higher probability

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.829
that a solar flare will knock out all of our
electronics and send us back to like 1860.

00:08:55.829 --> 00:09:04.160
So the point is that, first of all, there's
a lot of things going on to make up an object

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:05.670
that you don't detect with your vision.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:11.540
You never think that the reason the computer
works is because the sun is acting properly.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:17.120
But it is of course the case that endless
number of things have to be stabilized that

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:18.120
you don't see.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:22.590
And you might say, well the sun isn't part
of the computer but well, that's wrong.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.890
It's not part of the computer in some sense
when it's behaving, but as soon as it stops

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.520
behaving it's instantly part of the computer.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:36.490
So here we've got--you might say, well let's
say the power hadn't gone out and the computer

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:37.490
stopped working.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:42.680
Well then you might think, well I'll never
buy that brand again.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:51.140
And maybe the brand is associated with operation
in some country where corruption is rife and

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.700
things are made improperly and the parts don't
work very well.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.900
So you might think, well I made a stupid purchase
decision.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:01.340
And of course that's connected to the political
system of that place and the economy.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:06.050
And then weird little things can happen down
at the micro level as well as the macro level.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:12.650
So computer chips have now got so small--you
know they have little wires in them right,

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:16.270
the chips--the wires are now so small that
because of quantum uncertainty.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:20.890
So quantum uncertainty basically tells you
that you can't really tell where an electron

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.130
is if you want to know how fast it's going.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.320
And you can't tell how fast it's going if
you want to know where it is.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.290
But more than that it says you can't really
know where the thing is, and so what that

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.690
means is that once you get your wire short
enough, some of the electrons might not be

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:38.150
in the wires where they're supposed to be,
and they actually cause short circuits.

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:40.170
So that's another issue.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:43.779
There's other levels of reality that are micro
levels that you can't detect at all.

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:47.960
So there's macro levels that are way beyond
you, you know, political systems, economic

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:51.800
systems, biological systems, physical systems
outside of that.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:55.920
And then there are these Microsystems layered
all the way down, and things can go wrong

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:01.870
at any one of those levels and a lot of that
is invisible to us.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:04.640
The same thing happens when something goes
wrong with you.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.650
It's like, what's wrong with you?

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:10.680
Maybe you're malfunctioning at the molecular
level.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.370
Your DNA isn't working properly so you get
cancer.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:19.080
Or maybe some virus which is even smaller
than the DNA comes in and takes up residence

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.360
and that's really not so good.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.810
Or maybe you're eating something wrong and
you have an organ malfunction.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:29.340
Or maybe your family's screwed up and you're
stressed half to death and the reason they're

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:36.060
screwed up is because you live in, let's say
a society that's not friendly to your particular

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:41.370
immigrant type, and so the real reason that
you're sick is because of people's inability

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:42.670
to get along with one another.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.050
And so on and so forth and so on.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:48.830
And so god only knows where you're supposed
to focus.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.620
But, focus we do.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:59.191
And one of the things that motivation allows
you to do is to figure out what you're going

00:11:59.191 --> 00:12:00.191
to focus on.

00:12:00.191 --> 00:12:05.650
Now you think, god, there's all these things
to focus on.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:09.270
There's an infinite number of things to focus
on.

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:16.220
How in the world do you ever figure out how
to focus on anything?

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:23.280
Well the answer to that is evolution, fundamentally.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.660
You can view evolution in the following way.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:31.130
You can kind of think about the background
of reality--I think the best way to think

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:36.510
about it is as this interplay of patterns,
complex patterns sort of like music.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:41.160
And those patterns are always changing in
a sort of dynamic way, and they exist at multiple

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.600
levels, just like complex music does.

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:50.279
So then imagine that what you're trying to
do is to dance to the music, to align yourself

00:12:50.279 --> 00:12:51.279
to the music.

00:12:51.279 --> 00:12:54.080
And it keeps changing on you so, well, what
do you do?

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.120
Well if you can change then you can change
the music.

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:01.030
But if you can't change then that's it.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.190
You're not dancing anymore.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:05.750
Okay, so let's assume that there's two ways
of solving that problem.

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:08.720
One way is just sheer number.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:13.300
You're a single celled organism or a mosquito
and you don't have that much neurocapacity

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:17.170
for transformation and so you don't know what
the melody is going to be when you lay your

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:23.339
eggs if you're a mosquito and so you produce
like a million eggs and some of them can dance

00:13:23.339 --> 00:13:27.110
to the current beat and the others can't,
and so they all die.

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:30.860
And so that's what happens over the course
of evolution for a very very long time.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:36.839
The melody keeps changing and then what creatures
do is propagate a whole variety of variants

00:13:36.839 --> 00:13:43.320
of themselves, and in the case of small animals
or fish even or crayfish or that sort of thing,

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:48.600
they just overproduce offspring like mad in
hope that one of them can dance to the current

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.740
tune.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:53.520
And then you know, we get three and a half
billion years later, roughly speaking, here

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.440
we all are and what we've got is this dynamic
nervous system.

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:04.990
And it enables us to take an external pattern
and map it to a neurological pattern, or multiple

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:10.050
neurological patterns--because we can interpret
things, right--and then to output that to

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:11.640
multiple behavioural patterns.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:17.720
And so in some sense, what we've done is we're
internalised the process of evolutionary variation

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:19.410
into ourselves.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:24.240
And the more complex, roughly speaking, the
more layers your nervous system has--imagine

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.540
there's a pattern on your skin, and then that's
interpreted by a pattern of nerves, and then

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:34.040
those nerves have an output, they're sensory
nerves we'll say, and then those have an output

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.810
to motor nerves, and then those have an output
to muscles.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:38.540
That's you, roughly.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:41.630
You're maybe a four-layer thing in that regard.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:47.030
But then you grow all these extra layers of
neural tissue, so that the layers are hundreds

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:49.450
of cells thick.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:50.450
Hundreds of layers thick.

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:55.820
And then you can map a whole bunch of patterns
in the external world onto a whole bunch of

00:14:55.820 --> 00:15:00.040
patterns in the neural world and then onto
a whole bunch of patterns in the motor world,

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:03.420
and so that makes you dynamically adaptable.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:08.550
But you learned all that through this incredibly
painful process of evolution which basically

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:15.060
consisted of the death of like 99.999 percent
of everything that ever lived.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:17.800
Species and individuals alike.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.010
Now, why is that relevant?

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.970
Well, how do you figure out how to focus in
on something?

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:31.470
Well, a big part of that is actually--let's
see how we do it.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.110
Well first of all, what are you focusing in
on here?

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:37.780
Well not poisonous snakes, and rampaging lions
and chimpanzees.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.600
Because they're not in here.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:44.200
And the reason they're not in here, roughly
speaking, is because you live in a culture

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:47.750
that's very very highly advanced and it's
got walls like mad.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:51.000
This is a--Carcassonne I think, a French city,
medieval city.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.060
It still exists.

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:53.230
Beautiful city.

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:55.420
You see how the medieval people solved this
problem.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:57.470
It's like, well you can't deal with much.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:58.470
Well what do you do?

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.279
Build some walls, keep a bunch of stuff out.

00:16:00.279 --> 00:16:04.160
You don't have to worry about the rampaging
barbarians then because they just can't get

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:05.160
in.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:09.450
And so a lot of the reason that you can handle
the insane complexity of the world is because

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:10.720
it's not even neurological.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.899
It has virtually nothing to do with your physiology.

00:16:13.899 --> 00:16:19.220
It's just that you're in a university.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:23.740
And the university is protected by the city
and the city is protected by the province

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:28.140
and the province is protected by the country
and the country is protected by all of its

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.880
multiple entanglements.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:34.950
You're inside walls inside walls inside walls
inside walls and so on.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:36.459
Your world's pretty simple.

00:16:36.459 --> 00:16:40.770
You can just sit there in relative warmth
and comfort and not have to worry about anything.

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:44.089
And so there's one solution: there's a cultural
solution.

00:16:44.089 --> 00:16:48.550
Then inside the cultural solution obviously
there's architectural solutions of various

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:49.740
sorts.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:53.600
And then there's political and economic institutions
that are pretty stable, and they keep you

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.339
from having to deal with chaos.

00:16:55.339 --> 00:17:00.700
I mean you can see that chaos breaking out
a little bit in the previous weeks, in the

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:01.720
American election.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:06.659
Because the trump people and the left wingers
are going at it at the Trump rallies.

00:17:06.659 --> 00:17:12.309
And you can see that's just tinder, that could
just blow up at any point and all of that

00:17:12.309 --> 00:17:17.529
protection that's there because all of these
very complex games that we've agreed to play--like

00:17:17.529 --> 00:17:21.339
the democracy game--that just all flies by
the wayside.

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:25.029
And all of a sudden you have to contend with
what people are like, and that is not something

00:17:25.029 --> 00:17:26.360
you want to do.

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:30.779
There's people, man--they're capable of anything.

00:17:30.779 --> 00:17:33.130
Which is good, but also really bad.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:36.280
Well then the next thing is, you've got a
body.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.700
Now that's where the evolutionary process
really kicks in.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:45.929
And so the reason your body is the way it
is, roughly speaking, is because it's mimicked

00:17:45.929 --> 00:17:47.510
the external environment.

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:53.060
Just like Piaget talks about children using
their bodies to mimic the external environment

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:59.450
voluntarily, you know, your body is set up
through this evolutionary process so that

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:03.840
you can focus on precisely that subset of
things that seems to keep something like you

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:08.090
going for long enough for you to make another
one of something that's more or less like

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:09.390
you.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:12.929
And it only really works for about a hundred
years.

00:18:12.929 --> 00:18:15.120
That's pretty much where it tops out.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.630
And so you don't solve the problem very well.

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:21.680
You don't solve the problem of radical complexity
very well.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:27.010
But you solve it well enough so that you live
long enough to reproduce, speaking strictly

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:28.010
biologically.

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.150
And that appears to be good enough.

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:35.750
And part of the reason why you seem to expire,
because you might think, well if I--why don't

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:39.550
people just live for like 500 years instead
of 100 years?

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:46.870
Because you'd think that someone who could
continue to reproduce for 200 years instead

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:51.930
of 100 years would have it all over for someone
who could only manage it for 50 years, say.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:57.929
Turns out that your genes have a better time
of it if you just put them into a new body

00:18:57.929 --> 00:19:01.681
and shuffle off this mortal coil so you're
not using up too many resources while your

00:19:01.681 --> 00:19:05.350
children and grandchildren are trying to survive.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:09.850
And so that's an evolutionary solution.

00:19:09.850 --> 00:19:12.390
Death is not technically inevitable.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:14.690
There are creatures that are roughly immortal.

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:17.390
Goldfish, for example, which is really annoying.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:19.000
It's like, really?

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Goldfish?

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.680
Because they're a carp and carp basically
just keep growing.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:29.530
It isn't obvious that they senesce or age--they
get killed because they get diseases and these

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:34.340
sort of thing, but there are 300 or 400 year
old carp.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:37.770
And some turtles seem to not age really.

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:44.650
And then of course your DNA has been there
ever since life started, so it doesn't really

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:45.880
age either.

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:47.900
And it corrects itself.

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.180
So things can last a very very long time.

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:51.880
But not us.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:57.370
Anyways, we're put inside this body that frames
things for us and starts narrowing things

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:58.370
down.

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:05.600
And our psychophysiological selves have a
limited range of interests and desires and

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:10.610
needs--however you want to construe that--and
then that gets instantiated more into the

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:12.660
nervous system.

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:15.870
It's a mistake to think--people always think
your brain is in your head, and that's really

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:17.720
not a very bright way of thinking about it.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:22.520
I don't know exactly why we think that way
because, well there's the nervous system--you

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.800
can see it right there.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:29.179
There's a lot of your nervous system that's
not in your head.

00:20:29.179 --> 00:20:32.600
The motor and sensory systems are distributed
throughout your body.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:37.820
Your spine is smart enough so that if it gets
severed and we put you over top of a treadmill--suspend

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:39.400
you--you can walk.

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:42.770
You don't know you're walking and you can't
control it, but your legs will do a perfectly

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:46.110
good job of walking without you being involved
at all.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:49.880
So your spine is brain tissue for all intents
and purposes.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:52.760
Your brain is distributed through your body.

00:20:52.760 --> 00:21:00.530
And so, it's evolved so that it's pretty good
at going around specifying what it needs.

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:11.930
And so we talked about the hypothalamus the
other day and how it accounts for what I called

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:15.980
sub-personalities that are motivated towards
particular ends.

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:21.430
And those ends are obviously the things that
we would identify as fundamental biological

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:23.360
necessities.

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:30.049
But then they transform themselves as we interact
socially into more and more complex fundamental

00:21:30.049 --> 00:21:33.330
biological necessities.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:38.450
It's a tricky thing because people talk about
needs as if they're biologically instantiated,

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:41.500
like they're the fundamental building elements
of motivation.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:47.880
But it's not really that obvious, because
you might say, well being hungry is a biological

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:50.510
need but being a doctor isn't.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:56.070
Well, not really because if you do end up
being a physician you pretty much solve the

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:58.830
food acquisition problem permanently, right.

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:02.510
So it's just a higher order manifestation
of the same thing.

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:06.929
And it's higher order because instead of just
eating once you eat every day for the next

00:22:06.929 --> 00:22:07.929
40 years.

00:22:07.929 --> 00:22:11.610
And instead of just feeding you, you feed
you and your family and maybe some other people

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:12.610
too.

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:17.130
So, thinking of that as something that isn't
rooted in biologically is not accurate.

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:21.880
It's kind of a continuous complexification.

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:31.510
From the simple local time-bound immature
subpersonalities that can only fulfill themselves

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:36.630
in this moment and with help, to the development
of those circuits--which I would say is your

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:41.500
personality--that are capable of providing
you with what you need in order to live and

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:44.820
to be attracted to other people across very
large spans of time.

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:45.960
It's like a tree, you know.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:48.210
It's a like a tree expanding upward.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:51.690
It's a good metaphor.

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:54.970
Alright, so that's fine.

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:58.240
Motivation sets up your perceptual frame.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:03.820
And so people often talk about motivation
as if it's a drive or a need--not as if those

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:05.870
terms are particularly well-defined.

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:11.720
A drive I suppose is a deterministic sequence
of motor outputs, something like that.

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:18.309
But it's not accurate because the motivation
actually specifies what you look at.

00:23:18.309 --> 00:23:21.809
It specifies right what you see.

00:23:21.809 --> 00:23:27.380
And then it specifies as well what you're
going to respond to emotionally.

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:34.000
So just imagine this, say I'd offered a 10,000
dollar prize to the person who counts the

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.610
number of basketballs correctly.

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:41.340
And then you're doing your best to count the
number of basketballs in the monkey illusion

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:45.500
and then somebody stands up in front of you
like this, and blocks your view.

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:48.670
Well what emotion are you going to feel?

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:50.929
It's a negative emotion, right?

00:23:50.929 --> 00:23:51.929
In all likelihood.

00:23:51.929 --> 00:23:55.640
And so that might be anxiety, because you're
not going to win.

00:23:55.640 --> 00:24:01.200
It might be fear--it's like anxiety because
you're not going to win, but also like--fear

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:04.480
because what the hell is this crazy person
doing.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:05.480
Anger.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:10.340
Right, that's another negative emotion, although
there's a positive attack element to it.

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:17.590
Frustration, disappointment, maybe some grief--a
whole undifferentiated mess of negative emotions.

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:18.590
Well why?

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:27.480
Well, in some sense the motivational system
specifies the map and the goal.

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:30.450
It's a little more complicated than that because
it also specifies where you are.

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:33.410
But you can think about it as a map with a
destination.

00:24:33.410 --> 00:24:37.929
That's why stories are like that, because
we inhabit them.

00:24:37.929 --> 00:24:39.870
A map's like that all the time.

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:46.000
And then what emotions do, roughly speaking,
is tell you whether or not things are going

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.380
the way you want them to as you're on that
path.

00:24:49.380 --> 00:24:57.530
And so the emotions in some sense have to
have a motivational specification before they're

00:24:57.530 --> 00:24:58.530
properly functional.

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.530
Like, what should you get angry at.

00:25:00.530 --> 00:25:05.390
Well generally you get angry at things that
either interfere with your progression towards

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:13.710
a valued goal or upset the entire sub-personality
that contains the goal itself.

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:21.549
That's a more--what could you call it--that's
a more serious failure.

00:25:21.549 --> 00:25:25.419
It's one to fail when you're trying to do
something, it's another thing to fail so hard

00:25:25.419 --> 00:25:27.590
you have to give up the whole project.

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:31.530
We would rather just fail at some sub-element
than have to give up the whole project, right.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:37.160
It's one thing to fail an exam, it's another
thing to fail at university.

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:42.410
And I think the reason for that--I thought
about that for a long time--why is it worse

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:46.310
to fail out of university, assuming you want
to be in university, than it is to fail an

00:25:46.310 --> 00:25:47.310
exam?

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:49.580
How does your brain compute that?

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:53.650
Because you can't tell all the consequences,
right, so it's not self evident.

00:25:53.650 --> 00:26:02.000
But it seems to me that it's something like,
you think about these functional sub-personalities

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:08.670
as having a temporal and spatial range of
application.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:12.730
So if you're a university student you're sort
of stabilized for four years.

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:15.360
Wherever you go, people say "what do you do?"

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:17.289
and you can say "I’m a university student."

00:26:17.289 --> 00:26:21.350
And everybody's happy about that.

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:27.380
From an economic perspective it's probably
worse than being unemployed, in some sense,

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:31.410
but you can't just go tell people that you're
unemployed and then you have a nice little

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:32.919
high-status slot in society.

00:26:32.919 --> 00:26:33.919
It doesn't work that way.

00:26:33.919 --> 00:26:37.770
But you can do that as a university student,
you know, so it fulfills your social obligations

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:43.250
and it makes you feel like you're doing something
at least vaguely useful--I mean apart from

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:45.490
the learning and all the other things that
are positive about it.

00:26:45.490 --> 00:26:51.870
So what happens is that that map basically
covers that space and time--the time is four

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:55.110
years and the space is everywhere you go during
those four years.

00:26:55.110 --> 00:27:00.910
Then if that map burns up it's like you're
exposed on all fronts for that entire four

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:01.910
year period.

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:05.780
And that might even be the two years previously.

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:11.090
For example--strange to think that, but if
you bail out of university half way through

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:18.500
your third year, you've also destructured
the map that you used to organize your memories,

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:21.130
in some sense, of the previous two and a half
years.

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:24.740
Because first of all they were the memories
of somewone who was doing just fine and going

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:28.270
through university, and now all of a sudden
they're the memories of someone who failed.

00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:30.350
Those aren't the same memories.

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:38.960
And so it's weird that an error can alter
the past, but it can and often does.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:43.740
I mean any relationship breakup that's of
any significance will do exactly the same

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:45.840
thing, especially if you were betrayed, right.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:50.789
It's like, it's not just the present and the
future that dissolve into chaos and take you

00:27:50.789 --> 00:27:52.429
on a little trip to the underworld.

00:27:52.429 --> 00:27:58.070
It's also your revision--the necessity of
revising your past.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:03.169
So anyways, so then I would say well--failing
a class--well the class restructures you in

00:28:03.169 --> 00:28:08.200
some sense and focuses you for a certain subset
of the general going-to-university map.

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:14.090
So it's going to be less traumatizing to do
poorly on a class than it is to fail out of

00:28:14.090 --> 00:28:21.260
university because the amount of space and
time that those respective maps covers differs.

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:28.000
So what you're trying to do always is to lay
out a functional game--that's a good way of

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:31.309
thinking about it--on the space-time territory
that you inhabit.

00:28:31.309 --> 00:28:36.960
And if you understand that then you can start
to understand a little bit about emotions.

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:45.750
Now, okay, I have to put a little coda in
there and that's: motivations and emotions

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.000
are not technical terms.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:50.100
They're sort of like id and ego.

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:54.250
They're terms that you can use to structure
a debate.

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.420
I showed you the hypothalamus and you see
it's made out of all those little nuclei--they're

00:28:57.420 --> 00:29:01.370
not the same, so you can't say that every
part of the hypothalamus is doing something

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:04.880
that you can fit into a class: motivation.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:12.350
And motivations and emotions overlap, so,
I would say hunger--some of them oversee more

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:15.450
purely motivation.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:21.309
If you have an unsettling altercation with
someone, it tends not to make you hungry.

00:29:21.309 --> 00:29:26.909
Whereas it might make you angry, it might
make you happy, it might make you sad.

00:29:26.909 --> 00:29:32.120
So hunger is something that seems to pop up
one of these maps and then emotions guide

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:35.100
your way through it or tell you where you
are on it.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:41.600
But then you have complex motivation-emotions
like pain, and also anger.

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:45.140
Because pain has a goal in it, right--get
away.

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:50.309
Or stop, or get away--it's usually get away--but
it also has an emotional feeling, right.

00:29:50.309 --> 00:29:51.789
It feels bad.

00:29:51.789 --> 00:29:54.559
So it feels like an emotion, pain.

00:29:54.559 --> 00:29:56.669
Grief is like pain.

00:29:56.669 --> 00:29:58.490
Disappointment is like pain.

00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:01.770
Loneliness is like pain.

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:05.860
And I mean this technically--all of those
things can be addressed rather successfully

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:07.350
with opiates, by the way.

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:09.250
So technically they're the same.

00:30:09.250 --> 00:30:13.030
But pain--is being hurt an emotion?

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:16.360
Well people will say that, "well I feel hurt."

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:17.360
Whatever.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:24.500
And anger is another one of those that's sort
of ambivalent because anger has a goal, right:

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.280
remove obstacle.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:27.360
Remove or destroy obstacle.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:29.430
In a sense, that's the goal of anger.

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:32.289
But people talk about it as an emotion.

00:30:32.289 --> 00:30:34.520
So we're oversimplifying.

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:39.190
But it doesn't matter because it's a useful
oversimplification and that's sort of what

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:41.360
a theory is: a useful oversimplification.

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:49.799
Alright, so 
I kind of laid out what I think are the basic

00:30:49.799 --> 00:30:51.830
motivations there.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:31:00.140
Hunger, thirst, pain, anger, thermoregulation--you
don't want to be too hot or too cold--panic

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:07.480
or escape, affiliation or care, sexual desire,
exploration, play.

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:12.909
And you can kind of divide those into self-maintenance
motivations and self-propagation motivations,

00:31:12.909 --> 00:31:15.110
roughly speaking.

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:16.850
Sort of just a way to keep track of them.

00:31:16.850 --> 00:31:20.070
And then emotions--so the hypothalamus is
involved in a lot of that.

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:24.590
It's not the only thing but it's involved
in a lot of that.

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:27.790
And so here's sort of how I've conceptualized
one of these maps.

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:29.840
And we've seen this thing before.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:35.400
You're at point A because if you're using
a map, or if you're in a game, there's a starting

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:40.570
point or you're a player--either way there's
you there--and then you're going somewhere

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:42.409
and you have to do something to get there.

00:31:42.409 --> 00:31:45.380
Okay, so that's the little map, and maybe
you have to go make a peanut butter sandwich

00:31:45.380 --> 00:31:49.409
or you have to go turn the thermostat down
or you need to call your friend or whatever.

00:31:49.409 --> 00:31:55.070
But it's the same kind of structure that you're
using in all those different situations.

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:56.799
And they switch, you know.

00:31:56.799 --> 00:31:58.679
Now you're finished being hungry.

00:31:58.679 --> 00:32:00.549
Now you're thirsty.

00:32:00.549 --> 00:32:02.419
Then you're lonesome.

00:32:02.419 --> 00:32:03.900
It's like Sisyphus.

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:07.429
It's one damn thing after another, roughly
speaking.

00:32:07.429 --> 00:32:08.960
And so that's what keeps you alive.

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.990
And you have to be chasing things all the
time because your natural tendency is to run

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.160
out of fuel and decay.

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:17.179
That's entropy--you have to fight that.

00:32:17.179 --> 00:32:21.309
And so that's a continual battle because you're
pretty organized and it's really hard for

00:32:21.309 --> 00:32:25.390
something that organized to just stay organized.

00:32:25.390 --> 00:32:28.830
It's a lot easier--there's a million ways
to mess up your room.

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:30.340
There's like one way to clean it.

00:32:30.340 --> 00:32:33.340
Or maybe 10, you get the point.

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:37.010
And it's the same with anything that's extraordinarily
complex.

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:41.770
There's not that many ways it can stay in
order and there's a lot of ways it can fall

00:32:41.770 --> 00:32:42.770
apart.

00:32:42.770 --> 00:32:43.940
That's why you're running all the time on
a treadmill.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:47.360
You're fighting the second--is it the second
law of thermodynamics.

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:49.240
Is entropy the second law of thermodynamics?

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:50.460
I think so.

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:54.409
That's a major one, so good luck in your scrap.

00:32:54.409 --> 00:33:00.750
Because you're going to need it.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:06.570
So these little underlying biological systems
pop up the primary maps and then those are

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:09.530
elaborated upwards into maps that are more
and more complicated.

00:33:09.530 --> 00:33:13.440
And the more and more complicated maps maybe
solve two problems at the same time for a

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:14.440
week.

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:17.529
Or they solve 10 problems at the same time
for 20 years.

00:33:17.529 --> 00:33:23.700
That's why you want a job or a career: a whole
bunch of problems are solved immediately as

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:25.510
soon as you're employed.

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:31.559
I mean, you have some other problems, but
that's inescapable anyways.

00:33:31.559 --> 00:33:34.560
Okay so you're inside one of these so you're
always motivated.

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:36.340
You're always running around after something.

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:42.210
And that's another thing to know too because
the basic state of human beings is not quiescence,

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:43.210
you know.

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:45.860
You're not napping in front of a fireplace.

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:50.710
If you're not pursuing something that you're
motivated to pursue, then generally what you're

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:54.950
doing if figuring out how to pursue other
things that you're motivated to pursue.

00:33:54.950 --> 00:33:55.950
And that's exploration.

00:33:55.950 --> 00:34:02.000
And so if you're not actively motivated by
biological necessity, we'll say for the sake

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:07.880
of simplicity, then the next motivation kicks
in, which is well, prepare for the next time

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:09.820
that you are so motivated.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:12.129
And that really accounts for our capacity
to explore.

00:34:12.129 --> 00:34:16.360
We're always zooming around the world trying
to figure out what to do with it next, you

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:17.360
know.

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:21.429
Acquiring more information or--even when we're
entertaining ourselves because we're almost

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:23.839
always looking at stories when we entertain
ourselves.

00:34:23.839 --> 00:34:28.849
We're still acquiring information that has
functional significance and so attractive

00:34:28.849 --> 00:34:34.119
to us that we find it innately rewarding just
to observe that sort of thing.

00:34:34.119 --> 00:34:38.850
It's even in strange situations, like people
will go to horror movies.

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:39.850
What's wrong with them?

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:42.310
Why do you want to go and get scared?

00:34:42.310 --> 00:34:43.310
Or disgusted?

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:46.929
Because that's horror, right--fear and disgust.

00:34:46.929 --> 00:34:50.540
Plain uncertainty horror movies like The Blair
Witch Project, that's pretty much all fear.

00:34:50.540 --> 00:34:55.080
And then the slasher movies and that sort
of thing, that's pretty much all disgust.

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:58.410
And you might say, well why bother exposing
yourself to that.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:02.460
And part of it is: to get over it.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:03.800
You know, it's exposure.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:08.740
Because life has terrifying elements and it
has disgusting elements, and you're going

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:14.830
to have to learn to maintain yourself in the
face of that.

00:35:14.830 --> 00:35:20.849
Certainly illness is going to challenge your
capacity to deal with disgust.

00:35:20.849 --> 00:35:27.660
There's any number of reasons to be terrified
so, you go to movies and you practice facing

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:28.660
it.

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:29.720
And you observe that you can.

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:35.030
I mean there's other motivations too, but
those are sort of the healthy ones that go

00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:39.480
along with people wanting to expose themselves
to dangerous situations.

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:44.330
So even in that you see exploration and preparation
for what's going to happen next.

00:35:44.330 --> 00:35:47.470
And the exploratory circuit is a very very
fundamental circuit.

00:35:47.470 --> 00:35:49.180
It's also hypothalamically mediated.

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:55.670
So, the way the hypothalamus works--it's quite
cool--is that, on one hand it pops up all

00:35:55.670 --> 00:36:00.680
these little maps that you can occupy that
have their core in, we'll say, biological

00:36:00.680 --> 00:36:06.040
necessity--so roughly Freud's id--but when
those things all shut down because they're

00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:12.770
satiated, that's a technical term by the way--satiated,
it means satisfied--it's a form of reward.

00:36:12.770 --> 00:36:19.680
So it's sort of how you feel after you've
eaten a good meal and you're in a warm room

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:25.040
and your friends are around you and maybe
you go to sleep if it's your family.

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:31.339
Because--I'm thinking of thanksgiving or something
like that--everything is just taken care of

00:36:31.339 --> 00:36:33.130
and so you fall asleep.

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:34.870
That's satiation.

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:38.490
You can see that in animals all the time because
they spend a lot of time satiated.

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:39.500
Dogs: they sleep a lot.

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:41.000
Cats: they sleep a lot.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:46.180
Because they've already attacked their cat
food and so that's pretty much it for the

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:47.230
day.

00:36:47.230 --> 00:36:52.630
Human beings, we tend not so much to enter
into states of satiation.

00:36:52.630 --> 00:37:01.020
Now what happens when you're in one of those
little units--one of those little motivated

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:05.040
things--there's a couple of things that can
happen.

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:07.330
[Muttering].

00:37:07.330 --> 00:37:42.000
Okay so we talked about motivations as sort
of setting up this little frame.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:44.660
And then we can talk about emotions.

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:50.710
So, there you are going somewhere.

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:55.040
And there's this little straight line that'll
take you there, this nice efficient way to

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:56.210
get there.

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:58.080
Now why do you want to do it efficiently?

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:03.090
Well, because you use up the least amount
of resources like that so you can maintain

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:06.480
some resources for doing the next thing you
need to do.

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:08.480
So you care about efficiency and simplicity.

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:11.990
So you want to go like the crow flies to the
destination.

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:15.070
So then, what happens along the way?

00:38:15.070 --> 00:38:19.260
Well, let's say you just get to go straight.

00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:23.780
Well basically what happens is that you're
motivated by your perception of the goal or

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:28.990
intermediary goals because the goal can be
linked together, and then as long as things

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:34.940
are going according to plan, as you move towards
the goal in an untrammelled way you're going

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:36.660
to feel mild positive emotion.

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:42.420
And the reason for that is that you're turning
the environment--the environment almost never

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:43.940
has...

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:45.530
most of it (the environment) is irrelevant.

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:48.200
The rest of it has a positive or negative
valence.

00:38:48.200 --> 00:38:53.230
And if things are going according to plan
it has a mild positive valence, right.

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:57.390
You think things are okay, and really what
you're doing is you're observing two things.

00:38:57.390 --> 00:39:02.560
You're observing that the things that should
be happening, if you know what you're doing

00:39:02.560 --> 00:39:05.780
while you're acting, are happening.

00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:12.359
And so each step forward is an indication
that you're getting closer to your goal.

00:39:12.359 --> 00:39:16.180
That's incentive reward.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.540
And that's the sort of reward that you actually
think of as fun.

00:39:19.540 --> 00:39:25.070
An incentive reward is produced by activation
of the exploratory system that's in the hypothalamus--it

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:29.260
has its roots in the hypothalamus--and that's
the dopaminergic system.

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:32.880
And that's the system that cocaine and amphetamines
and all those drugs that people really like

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:37.800
to take hyperactivates, making them feel like
they're doing something important and useful.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:40.570
Even though, in all likelihood, they're not.

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:44.630
So the dopaminergic drugs in some sense hijack
your incentive reward systems.

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:49.460
And it's incentive reward because you're incentivized
to continue moving forward.

00:39:49.460 --> 00:39:55.640
Anything that you look at that makes you want
to touch it or move forward to it basically

00:39:55.640 --> 00:39:59.490
activates the dopaminergic system.

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:05.690
Now, so you say, well what happens when you're
on your way to somewhere.

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:10.539
Well, roughly what happens is neutral things,
which is okay.

00:40:10.539 --> 00:40:11.740
You can ignore them.

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:13.530
Positive things or negative things.

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:17.500
Positive things happen when things are going
according to plan or even better than according

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:18.500
to plan.

00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:22.070
You're walking to school, it's raining, somebody
you know stops and offers you a life.

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:25.770
It's like, well you're doing okay getting
to school and that was fine, but all of a

00:40:25.770 --> 00:40:28.450
sudden you're getting there a little faster
and you're not getting wet.

00:40:28.450 --> 00:40:30.460
So that cheers you up.

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:35.240
It's an indication that you're able to save
resources while you're moving towards a goal--dopaminergically

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:36.240
mediated.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:40.721
You know, and then we can imagine a different
scenario where you're walking to school in

00:40:40.721 --> 00:40:45.099
the rain and someone drives by you, maybe
it's even the same friend, and they don't

00:40:45.099 --> 00:40:48.690
see you and there's a puddle right beside
you and not only do they not stop but they

00:40:48.690 --> 00:40:54.220
completely cover you with oily and frigid
water, and so that's not so good.

00:40:54.220 --> 00:40:58.190
You're angry about that and upset.

00:40:58.190 --> 00:40:59.630
Why?

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:02.490
Well, you might say, "my day is ruined."

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:04.350
What does that mean exactly?

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:09.410
Well it means that you had a little map on
top of your day and you were hoping that the

00:41:09.410 --> 00:41:13.070
day and the map would correspond, and then
all of a sudden the map burned up and god

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:15.520
only knows what's going to happen to the day.

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:18.980
And so it's like the bottom has dropped out
of your map.

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:22.430
And so then you have to worry about your clothes
and catching cold and all those things.

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:26.270
And turning around and going home and what
it'll mean if you miss class and so on and

00:41:26.270 --> 00:41:27.270
so forth.

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.550
So basically that's the domain of chaos that
we talked about.

00:41:30.550 --> 00:41:35.070
So that's a negative thing, but it's also--the
thing about negative things is they're sort

00:41:35.070 --> 00:41:38.740
of hard to disentangle from unexpected things.

00:41:38.740 --> 00:41:42.300
You know, because most of the things that
happen to you that are negative are also unexpected

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:46.849
because you don't go around trying to make
negative things happen.

00:41:46.849 --> 00:41:52.080
So well, what happens when something negative
happens?

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.430
Well, you get a negative emotion.

00:41:54.430 --> 00:41:58.260
Pain, anxiety, disgust.

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:01.710
Those seem to be the big three.

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:03.880
And they're mediated by different systems.

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:06.579
We don't understand the disgust system very
well.

00:42:06.579 --> 00:42:12.920
It doesn't load with neuroticism because all
the other negative emotions load with trait

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:13.920
neuroticism.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:19.040
Pain--pain seems to be what you experience
if your receptive surfaces are--you know if

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:26.589
your physiological self is subject to stimulation
of an intensity that's sufficient either to

00:42:26.589 --> 00:42:30.339
damage it or to damage the receptors.

00:42:30.339 --> 00:42:32.290
You know, but pain is complicated.

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:35.420
It's not just physical pain from physical
damage.

00:42:35.420 --> 00:42:38.930
It depends on how you define physical damage.

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:43.710
If someone dies, that's pain.

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:46.220
If you're alone and isolated, that's pain.

00:42:46.220 --> 00:42:49.000
If you're depressed, that's pain.

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:53.210
If you're frustrated or disappointed, that's
pain.

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:57.270
Frustrated might mean something gets in the
way of you moving towards a goal, and disappointment

00:42:57.270 --> 00:43:00.770
might mean the whole damn structure just collapsed
on you.

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:03.560
Either way, that's pain.

00:43:03.560 --> 00:43:09.079
Now the behaviourists would have called that
an unconditioned--like a painful stimulus--they

00:43:09.079 --> 00:43:11.130
would've called that an unconditioned stimulus.

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:14.750
You don't have to learn for it to be bad.

00:43:14.750 --> 00:43:16.930
It's sort of built in bad.

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:22.290
And they would think about anxiety in response
to the unexpected as learned.

00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:30.170
The behaviourists would think that you need
to learn what predicts something painful.

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:34.400
And if you learn that something predicts something
painful then you get anxious to it or afraid

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:37.550
of it.

00:43:37.550 --> 00:43:40.000
But it turns out that it's more complicated
than that.

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:44.640
So what the behaviourists would think is that
pain in some sense is the fundamental negative

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:46.410
emotion.

00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:52.940
And then maybe you're a kid and you stand
up underneath a table and you whack your head

00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:54.240
on it when you're learning to walk.

00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:58.570
And then when you go under the table now you
get anxious because you've learned that if

00:43:58.570 --> 00:44:01.930
you stand up under the table, you bang your
head and that hurts.

00:44:01.930 --> 00:44:04.530
So anxiety under those circumstances is a
learned behaviour.

00:44:04.530 --> 00:44:06.480
It's a conditioned response.

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:07.480
Got it?

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:13.200
Okay, but it's more complicated than that
because anxiety can also be an unlearned response.

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:18.880
So if you just encounter something you've
never seen before, it's going to make you

00:44:18.880 --> 00:44:19.880
anxious.

00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:22.800
There's actually a circuit that mediates anxiety.

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:26.230
It's not just a secondary derivation of the
pain circuitry.

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:28.240
It's its own little unit.

00:44:28.240 --> 00:44:34.420
And it's sort of like you've figured out over
time--evolutionary time--not to get hurt because

00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:35.760
you don't want to be damaged.

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:41.490
But then, you've learned how to detect the
probability that you will get hurt for so

00:44:41.490 --> 00:44:46.250
long that you've also evolved a system just
to respond to that.

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:50.770
And so some of that responds to things that
you have learned are dangerous.

00:44:50.770 --> 00:44:54.900
Some of it responds to things that are generally
just dangerous.

00:44:54.900 --> 00:45:04.099
Blood, spiders, insects of various sorts,
facial expressions that indicate fear or disgust

00:45:04.099 --> 00:45:12.210
or anger, broken limbs, bodies--especially
mutilated ones.

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:16.790
You know what's in horror movies, that's all
sort of primary fear stimuli, right.

00:45:16.790 --> 00:45:17.849
And it's built right into you.

00:45:17.849 --> 00:45:22.859
Now psychologists debate about whether it's
built in or whether you can just learn it

00:45:22.859 --> 00:45:24.079
really easily.

00:45:24.079 --> 00:45:25.829
But I would say that there's sort of a continuum.

00:45:25.829 --> 00:45:27.369
Some of it seems pretty damn built-in.

00:45:27.369 --> 00:45:30.530
Snake-fear, for example.

00:45:30.530 --> 00:45:33.540
Chimps who've never seen snakes, you bring
one into their cage--if it's a rubber snake--poof,

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:35.329
they hit the roof.

00:45:35.329 --> 00:45:36.570
And then they look at it.

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:41.060
And snakes in the wild, a chimp will go up
to a big snake in the wild and hoot at it.

00:45:41.060 --> 00:45:44.180
They have a special snake--they call it a
snake [rah?]--and they'll look at the thing,

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:48.200
and some of them will just look at the snake
for like 24 hours.

00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:51.720
So, partly they're afraid of it.

00:45:51.720 --> 00:45:52.760
Partly they want to explore it.

00:45:52.760 --> 00:45:57.670
So what they do, they get close to it and
the fear increases, and then they back up

00:45:57.670 --> 00:45:59.650
and the fear decreases and the curiosity increases.

00:45:59.650 --> 00:46:03.440
And so what happens is that they move back
and forth until they're right on the line

00:46:03.440 --> 00:46:05.980
where they're afraid, but they're curious.

00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:08.579
So then they're stuck, and they're looking
at it.

00:46:08.579 --> 00:46:13.500
And you experience that all the time actually,
because when you are engaged in something

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:16.920
that's meaningful, that's where you are.

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:19.470
And that's like the line between order and
chaos.

00:46:19.470 --> 00:46:21.660
You know, because you want to solve it--it's
important.

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:26.680
Maybe you're reading papers about some illness
that you hope to work on or study.

00:46:26.680 --> 00:46:29.540
It's like, well you're worried about the damn
illness, you know.

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:33.190
All you're doing is confronting it through
the papers so, you know, you're fairly well

00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:34.440
sheltered from it.

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:38.609
But you don't want it to exist and you'd rather
it wasn't around, but you're curious so you're

00:46:38.609 --> 00:46:39.880
going to get engrossed in it.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:44.599
And most of you, to the degree that you're
immersed in anything, are partly immersed

00:46:44.599 --> 00:46:47.490
because you perceive it as a problem.

00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:51.099
So that's like existential anxiety in a sense.

00:46:51.099 --> 00:46:53.380
It's just part of being.

00:46:53.380 --> 00:46:55.420
There's problems that have to be solved.

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:58.450
They produce anxiety, they're complicated.

00:46:58.450 --> 00:47:02.750
You find this--you find a way of approaching
them so that the anxiety doesn't overwhelm

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:07.260
you and so the curiosity is optimized--that's
an incentive reward activation--and then you're

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:08.260
awake.

00:47:08.260 --> 00:47:11.390
Because the anxiety keeps you awake and so
does the curiosity.

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:13.390
And you think, yeah, this is a good place
to be.

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:17.220
And it is a good place to be because you're
optimally protected right there and you're

00:47:17.220 --> 00:47:18.579
optimally learning.

00:47:18.579 --> 00:47:21.540
So yes, that's a good place to be.

00:47:21.540 --> 00:47:28.119
You can think about that as an answer to the
Nietzschean problem of how you create value.

00:47:28.119 --> 00:47:31.530
You don't exactly create it--you find it.

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:36.750
There's problems that need to be solved, they
automatically engage your emotions--both positively

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:40.119
and negatively--you've got to find the right
balance.

00:47:40.119 --> 00:47:42.460
And it's something like contending, you know.

00:47:42.460 --> 00:47:46.470
It seems to me like humans beings have to
have something to contend with, or they can't

00:47:46.470 --> 00:47:47.780
tolerate themselves.

00:47:47.780 --> 00:47:52.490
Because life seems like a stupid joke if you
don't have something worthwhile to do.

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.140
But you can have something worthwhile to do.

00:47:54.140 --> 00:47:58.490
And so then maybe it's not so stupid and absurd
and tragic.

00:47:58.490 --> 00:48:01.670
And that's at least a good way to think about
it.

00:48:01.670 --> 00:48:05.420
(00:48:00)
So you've got satiation on the one hand, on

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:09.670
the positive side, and the behaviourists would've
considered that an unconditioned response

00:48:09.670 --> 00:48:10.670
too.

00:48:10.670 --> 00:48:12.460
They really thought about that as a reward
initially.

00:48:12.460 --> 00:48:16.619
You know, so you take a rat--he's hungry--and
you feed him something: reward.

00:48:16.619 --> 00:48:24.329
Now Skinner, he used to train rats, right,
and he was a very--what would you call it--very

00:48:24.329 --> 00:48:27.110
well renowned behavioural psychologists.

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:29.990
And he could train rats to do damn near anything.

00:48:29.990 --> 00:48:37.190
And what he would do is starve them to three
quarters of their body weight--they were basically

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:38.190
hungry rats.

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:43.150
They were hungry lonesome rats because they
lived an isolated existence, which rats don't

00:48:43.150 --> 00:48:48.090
like, and then they were starved down to three
quarters of their body weight.

00:48:48.090 --> 00:48:52.640
When the behaviourists used rats as a model
for people, they actually used hungry lonesome

00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:56.760
rats as a model for rats as a model for people.

00:48:56.760 --> 00:48:58.740
And you might think, well what kind of model
is that?

00:48:58.740 --> 00:49:02.690
But it turns out it's a pretty good model,
because you're a lot more like a scared hungry

00:49:02.690 --> 00:49:05.350
rat than you'd like to admit.

00:49:05.350 --> 00:49:08.390
Which is partly why you can empathize with
one, right.

00:49:08.390 --> 00:49:11.400
Everybody's sitting there thinking "oh the
poor rats".

00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:13.960
By the way, the more you thought that, the
more agreeable you are.

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:17.120
So how many people were feeling pretty sorry
for those rats?

00:49:17.120 --> 00:49:18.120
Yeah.

00:49:18.120 --> 00:49:19.420
How about men?

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:23.069
How many men were feeling sorry for those
rats?

00:49:23.069 --> 00:49:25.099
Oh yeah, about three.

00:49:25.099 --> 00:49:31.170
There's a big gender difference in agreeableness
between men and women, by the way.

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:34.579
Just so you know--we'll talk about that more.

00:49:34.579 --> 00:49:39.741
So now the behaviourists also thought that
you have this primary set of rewards, call

00:49:39.741 --> 00:49:45.359
them unconditioned, and then you had to learn
that some things predicted them.

00:49:45.359 --> 00:49:52.750
Like, classically maybe, a rat would push
a lever and get a pellet.

00:49:52.750 --> 00:49:54.230
Soon he's pushing that lever like mad.

00:49:54.230 --> 00:49:56.440
He's pretty happy about pushing that lever.

00:49:56.440 --> 00:50:01.760
And he learned the conditioned association
between lever pushing and getting a reward.

00:50:01.760 --> 00:50:06.109
But it turns out that because that's been
around for so long, you also have a system

00:50:06.109 --> 00:50:07.109
for it.

00:50:07.109 --> 00:50:11.310
So it's not just a learned association to
what satiates you--it's a whole system.

00:50:11.310 --> 00:50:14.010
And that's the dopaminergic system.

00:50:14.010 --> 00:50:17.760
The serotonin system seems to be the thing
that satiates.

00:50:17.760 --> 00:50:22.260
So you know, if you have a big turkey dinner,
up goes the serotonin levels--you don't have

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:23.260
to do anything.

00:50:23.260 --> 00:50:28.510
And serotonin is a calming and regulating
neurochemical.

00:50:28.510 --> 00:50:33.730
Which is why antidepressants stop your neurons
from taking up serotonin before you've had

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:38.579
a real chance to use it to regulate your nervous
system.

00:50:38.579 --> 00:50:42.670
But dopamine tells you, hey, good things are
on their way.

00:50:42.670 --> 00:50:44.820
And that's pretty much what people run on.

00:50:44.820 --> 00:50:46.890
And you can think about that as hope.

00:50:46.890 --> 00:50:49.900
So incentive reward is sort of like hope,
or promise.

00:50:49.900 --> 00:50:52.160
I think promise is a better term.

00:50:52.160 --> 00:50:57.970
So there's pain and satiation, and those are
really ancient systems.

00:50:57.970 --> 00:51:05.650
And then there are newer systems that are
anxiety and promise.

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:10.490
And those are related to those more fundamental
negative and positive emotions.

00:51:10.490 --> 00:51:13.580
And those things regulate you while you're
on your path.

00:51:13.580 --> 00:51:18.599
Now, there's one more level of complication,
because you think, well what can happen on

00:51:18.599 --> 00:51:20.020
the way to your goal.

00:51:20.020 --> 00:51:21.020
Nothing?

00:51:21.020 --> 00:51:22.020
Irrelevant.

00:51:22.020 --> 00:51:28.079
Okay, so that's the irrelevance that enables
you to not see the gorilla.

00:51:28.079 --> 00:51:31.180
And almost everything around you is irrelevant.

00:51:31.180 --> 00:51:34.099
And I think that's partly why I think people
don't like to have their little maps of the

00:51:34.099 --> 00:51:36.060
world destroyed.

00:51:36.060 --> 00:51:41.150
Because if your map of the world is thoroughly
demolished everything becomes relevant.

00:51:41.150 --> 00:51:44.230
And you do not want everything to become relevant.

00:51:44.230 --> 00:51:46.290
It's just too much for you.

00:51:46.290 --> 00:51:50.540
So you wake up and you're naked and it's dark
in the middle of a jungle.

00:51:50.540 --> 00:51:51.980
It's like, everything's relevant!

00:51:51.980 --> 00:51:54.200
You're not going to like that.

00:51:54.200 --> 00:51:55.720
It's going to burn you out quick.

00:51:55.720 --> 00:52:01.290
And so most of the time you have to be protected
so that almost everything's irrelevant.

00:52:01.290 --> 00:52:06.980
And then you can focus on the few things that
you're capable of handling, and you do that.

00:52:06.980 --> 00:52:11.630
So you're in your little map, and then as
you move towards it your emotions play.

00:52:11.630 --> 00:52:15.300
And the negative emotions appear when things
are not going well and the positive emotions

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:17.280
appear when things are going well.

00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:20.099
That's basically how your emotions work.

00:52:20.099 --> 00:52:24.990
Now, you've got your satiation and your pain.

00:52:24.990 --> 00:52:28.280
Then you've got your threat and your promise.

00:52:28.280 --> 00:52:31.980
Then on top of that you've got a more complicated
thing which is novelty, because that's the

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:37.250
other thing that can happen when you're on
the way from point A to point B. Something

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:42.190
you didn't expect--and you don't understand.

00:52:42.190 --> 00:52:54.690
So 

00:52:54.690 --> 00:53:02.040
you're walking down the street and there's
someone laying there face-down on the sidewalk.

00:53:02.040 --> 00:53:08.589
And you know, they're dishevelled, and so
well, what's your emotional response?

00:53:08.589 --> 00:53:13.380
Well you didn't expect it, so you're going
to be taken aback a bit: that's anxiety.

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:17.609
It's like, you're moving towards your goal
and anxiety stops you.

00:53:17.609 --> 00:53:25.500
Now you can think of the space-time area around
that person as unexplored territory.

00:53:25.500 --> 00:53:28.910
Now you don't want any unexplored territory
in your map because god only knows what's

00:53:28.910 --> 00:53:30.990
going to happen there.

00:53:30.990 --> 00:53:33.190
So what're you supposed to do?

00:53:33.190 --> 00:53:36.370
Well maybe you cross the street--you think
well that person's drunk or maybe you think,

00:53:36.370 --> 00:53:38.160
well they're a homeless person.

00:53:38.160 --> 00:53:41.010
Maybe they're lying on a sleeping back--you
think they're just asleep.

00:53:41.010 --> 00:53:46.060
You're going to stop and then you're going
to observe, and then you're going to use little

00:53:46.060 --> 00:53:47.970
cues to try to put your map back together.

00:53:47.970 --> 00:53:51.420
And your map is, what’s going on here and
what should I do about it?

00:53:51.420 --> 00:53:54.930
And so, it's partly this interplay between
anxiety and curiosity.

00:53:54.930 --> 00:53:59.540
Now you can just step around it and continue,
but maybe that'll upset your moral map--what

00:53:59.540 --> 00:54:00.800
kind of person am I?

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:02.721
Maybe I should have done something about it.

00:54:02.721 --> 00:54:07.560
Or maybe you think, there's no damn way I'm
going to get involved in this because, who

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:09.700
knows what'll happen if I step in there.

00:54:09.700 --> 00:54:14.720
And you know, fair enough, because, who knows
what'll happen if you step in there.

00:54:14.720 --> 00:54:21.079
The point is that, it's like your map which
has made everything irrelevant has got a hole

00:54:21.079 --> 00:54:25.140
in it and now some of what's complicated is
shining through.

00:54:25.140 --> 00:54:28.880
And that thing that's shining through--so
that's like what the phenomenologists talked

00:54:28.880 --> 00:54:33.359
about as shining forth--that thing that's
shining through, it's like you don't know

00:54:33.359 --> 00:54:34.750
what to do about it.

00:54:34.750 --> 00:54:36.119
What should you do?

00:54:36.119 --> 00:54:42.470
Well, you should do what you do do, and what
you do is, you prepare to do everything.

00:54:42.470 --> 00:54:45.140
And that's an emotional response to novelty.

00:54:45.140 --> 00:54:50.109
Well, if you don't know what to do you should
prepare yourself for anything.

00:54:50.109 --> 00:54:52.829
And that's stress.

00:54:52.829 --> 00:54:55.230
You could prepare that something good will
happen.

00:54:55.230 --> 00:54:57.880
Let’s say you're in an ambivalent.

